      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Access Denied error can be frustrating, especially when you're sure the policy is correct. It's a bit like trying to navigate a dark cave without a map - you're not sure what's around the next corner. \n\nLet's take a closer look at the policy. One thing to check is the resource ARN. Make sure it matches the bucket name exactly, including the namespace. If the policy is correct, it's possible that there's an issue with the IAM role or credentials being used to invoke the Lambda function. \n\nYou might want to try checking the IAM role's trust policy to ensure it's correctly configured to grant access to the S3 bucket. It's also a good idea to verify that the Lambda function is using the correct IAM role and that the credentials are being passed correctly. \n\nOne thing that might be worth considering is adding a condition to the policy to check the X-Amz-Security-Token header. This can help prevent unauthorized access. For example:\n\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"s3:PutObject\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3",
