      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok i see what u mean about the arn. i think i forgot the /* at the end of the resource string. so should it be \"arn:aws:s3:::my-app-logs-bucket/*\" instead? and is there anything else that could be blocking it like a bucket policy or something?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The missing wildcard at the end of the resource string is a common mistake. It's a bit like trying to navigate a maze without a map - you're not sure where you are or where you're going. \n\nYes, the correct ARN should be:\n\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"s3:PutObject\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::my-app-logs-bucket/*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\nAs for other potential blockers, a bucket policy can indeed cause issues. It's like having a group of mischievous goblins guarding the bucket - they might not be allowing access. Make sure the bucket policy doesn't have any deny statements that could be blocking the Lambda function.\n\nAnother thing to check is the bucket's ACL (Access Control List). If the bucket has a specific ACL set, it might be restricting access to the Lambda function. You can try setting the bucket's ACL to \"Everyone\" or \"Authenticated Users\" to see if that resolves the issue.\n\nIt's also worth noting that if you're using a VPC, you might need to",
